const faqs = [
  { "q": "What is a plantation shutter?", "a": "A plantation shutter is a solid interior window covering built as a rigid panel: a frame around the window, vertical stiles and horizontal rails forming the panel, and wide horizontal louvers that pivot open and closed on a tilt rod. Unlike a blind or a shade, it is a fixed piece of joinery mounted to the window, closer to furniture than to fabric." },
  { "q": "Why are they called plantation shutters?", "a": "The name comes from the wide-louvered shutters used on large plantation houses in the American South and the Caribbean in the 18th and 19th centuries. Wide louvers moved more air and blocked more overhead sun than the narrow slats common in Europe at the time, which mattered in hot, humid climates. The name stuck to describe the wide-louver style rather than any particular manufacturer." },
  { "q": "What are plantation shutters made of?", "a": "Three common materials. Basswood is real hardwood, light and stable, and takes paint and stain well. Faux wood is solid PVC or vinyl, waterproof and the usual pick for bathrooms and kitchens. Composite is an engineered core wrapped in a hard finish, sitting between the two on price and weight. In Florida, moisture resistance drives the decision more than anywhere else." },
  { "q": "What louver size should I choose?", "a": "Louvers usually come in 2.5, 3.5, and 4.5 inch widths. Wider louvers mean fewer of them across the panel, so you get more open sightline and more light when they are tilted open. Narrower louvers give a more traditional look and suit smaller windows. For most modern rooms with a view worth keeping, 3.5 inch is the common middle ground and 4.5 inch is the choice when light matters most." },
  { "q": "What is the difference between plantation shutters and blinds?", "a": "A blind is a stack of thin slats on cords that raises and lowers, and it lives in the window opening as a separate object. A plantation shutter is a rigid panel hinged to a frame that is fixed to the window; it does not raise, it swings open. Shutters cost more, last far longer, and read as part of the architecture. Blinds are cheaper, faster to install, and easier to replace." },
  { "q": "Are plantation shutters worth it?", "a": "They make sense if you plan to stay in the home several years, because a good shutter lasts 15 to 20 years and the cost spreads out over that time. They are strong on sun-facing windows, bathrooms, and street-facing rooms where privacy matters. They are a weaker choice for rentals, homes you are selling soon, or rooms where you want the window to feel open and uninterrupted." },
  { "q": "How much do plantation shutters cost?", "a": "Custom plantation shutters generally run a few hundred to around a thousand dollars per window installed, depending on material, louver size, window shape, and how the panels are configured. Faux wood on a standard window sits at the lower end; basswood on a tall or specialty-shaped opening sits higher. Any real number has to come from a measurement." },
  { "q": "Do plantation shutters need much maintenance?", "a": "Mostly dusting. Every louver is a flat surface that collects dust, so a microfiber duster or a vacuum brush attachment once a month keeps them looking new. Faux wood can be wiped down with a damp cloth, which is why it is the standard pick for bathrooms and kitchens. There are no cords to replace and no fabric to fade." }
];
